soft_max of a fully masked row gives 0 on the CPU backend and on OpenCL

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

Files:
    ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ops.cpp                    the CPU soft_max
    ggml/src/ggml-opencl/kernels/softmax_f32.cl  the four OpenCL kernels, one for each
    ggml/src/ggml-opencl/kernels/softmax_f16.cl  shape and mask type
    ggml/src/ggml-opencl/kernels/softmax_4_f32.cl
    ggml/src/ggml-opencl/kernels/softmax_4_f16.cl
    tests/test-backend-ops.cpp                   test_soft_max_masked_row

One idea, three backends. This patch covers the CPU backend and OpenCL. The
Hexagon DSP gets the same result in
patches/fuzz-ops/0009-hexagon-htp-soft-max-of-a-fully-masked-row-gives-0.patch,
which the series applies later: that patch changes hvx_fast_softmax_f32, a
function that the packed softmax kernel of patches/hexagon-kernels/0007 creates.
Thus it cannot come before that kernel.

The CPU backend

When the mask of SOFT_MAX is -inf in each column of a row, and the sink is
missing or also -inf, the maximum of the row is -inf. The CPU backend then
calculates exp(-inf - (-inf)), which is NaN, and each value of the row becomes
NaN. With GGML_CPU_VALUE_ASSERTS, a debug build stops at the assert
"sum > 0.0". With a finite sink, the same row gives 0 in each column.

This patch writes 0 into each column of such a row, and it does not calculate
the exponentials. This is the result of the same row with a finite sink, and
the result of a flash attention row with no unmasked position.

OpenCL

The four soft_max kernels calculate exp(-inf - (-inf)) in each column of such a
row, which is NaN. This patch writes 0 into each column after the reduction of
the maximum, in soft_max_f32, soft_max_f16, soft_max_4_f32 and soft_max_4_f16.

The test

test_soft_max_masked_row of test-backend-ops covers the two backends. The mask is
-inf in each column of the first and the last row of each matrix, and 0 in the
other rows. The cases have three shapes, an F32 or an F16 mask, and a sink or no
sink. The comparison of test-backend-ops fails on a NaN of either backend. With
-b CPU, the CPU backend is compared with itself, thus the NaN check tests the CPU
result.

The app graphs do not reach this condition: their masks have no fully masked row.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ops.cpp b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ops.cpp
index c8f0962..3f63b7a 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ops.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ops.cpp
@@ -5672,6 +5672,14 @@ static void ggml_compute_forward_soft_max_f32(
                     max = MAX(max, sk[i02]);
                 }
 
+                // A fully masked row (every value and the sink are -inf) has no finite value, thus
+                // exp(x - max) would be exp(-inf + inf) = NaN. Its result is 0 in every column, the
+                // same result as a flash attention row with no unmasked position.
+                if (max == -INFINITY) {
+                    memset(dp, 0, ne00*sizeof(float));
+                    continue;
+                }
+
                 ggml_float sum = ggml_vec_soft_max_f32(ne00, dp, wp, max);
                 GGML_CPU_VALUE_ASSERT(sum > 0.0);
 
